course: dictionary
lesson: 5913
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word marking has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around it. «The zebra crossing has bold white markings across the road.» Here markings are the painted lines on the road, not marks on paper.
explain: «She spent the whole evening on the marking.» Marking here means a teacher checking and scoring pupils' work. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it makes sense.
explain: In sport, marking means staying close to a player of the other team so they cannot get the ball. As a verb, marking means putting a mark on something. The same word can be a job, a pattern or a game move, so always check the clues.
fact: Marking is a noun, and it is also the -ing form of the verb mark.
fact: «Markings» are the lines, spots or patterns on an animal, road, plane or object.
fact: «Adult blue tangs are blue with black markings and a yellow tail.» Their markings are part of how they look.
fact: «Road signs and markings are a language»: lines on the road show where cars stop and where they may not park.
fact: «Telling the teacher about a marking mistake in your favour shows integrity.» Here marking means a teacher's checking of your work, and integrity means being honest.
exercise: «The zebra crossing has bold white markings across the road.» What are the markings? | Marks a teacher has made on a test | A kind of fish with a yellow tail | *The painted lines that keep people safe | Stripes on a tiger's back only
exercise: «She spent the whole evening on the marking.» What does marking mean here? | Writing a long story | Staying close to a player of the other team | Painting a wall in bright colours | *Checking and scoring pupils' work
exercise: Sam kept close to the striker all game, marking her. What does marking mean in sport? | Painting lines on the pitch | Cheering loudly for her team | *Staying close to a player of the other team to stop them | Checking the score at half time
test: «Leopards have rosettes, rose-shaped rings of dark spots with a lighter middle.» What are rosettes? | A kind of tree | Five-finger prints on the leopard's skin | Dark spots that a leopard gets when it is older | *The markings on a leopard's coat
test: «Self-marking is an honour system: it only works with trust.» What does self-marking mean? | A teacher checks the work with a red pen | Pupils paint their books | Pupils draw marks on the board | *Pupils check and score their own work
exam: «An honest 8 out of 10 beats a fake 10.» Here, what is the marking about? | The painted lines on a road | *The score given for work, where honesty matters more | Staying close to a player in a game | The spots on a leopard's coat
